# MjengoOS — environment template
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored — never commit real secrets).

# SQLite database file (Prisma). The relative path resolves from prisma/
# (where schema.prisma lives) — file:../db/custom.db = <repo>/db/custom.db
# (db/ is gitignored), matching the README quickstart.
DATABASE_URL=file:../db/custom.db

# NextAuth — REQUIRED. Stable secret for JWT session-cookie encryption.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32   (or: openssl rand -base64 32)
# Changing it invalidates every existing session (users sign in again).
# Production fails CLOSED at boot without a ≥ 32-char secret
# (src/backend/lib/next-auth-guard.ts), and docker-compose refuses to start
# with no value at all. The placeholder below is deliberately TOO SHORT so
# an unedited copy of this template fails closed instead of silently
# signing sessions on a guessable value.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=change-me

# NextAuth — public base URL.
# • Local dev / sandbox preview (reverse proxy): leave UNSET. With
#   AUTH_TRUST_HOST=1, next-auth v4 derives the origin from the request's
#   x-forwarded-host/-proto headers, so sign-in redirects, callback URLs and
#   cookie origins always target the host the user actually browses. Pinning
#   it to localhost breaks sign-in through the preview gateway.
# • Self-hosting on a fixed public domain: set https://your-domain.example
# NEXTAUTH_URL=

# NextAuth — trust the reverse proxy's forwarded host/proto headers.
# REQUIRED behind the sandbox preview gateway (v4 detectOrigin returns
# NEXTAUTH_URL unless VERCEL/AUTH_TRUST_HOST is set — unset both and every
# origin silently degrades to http://localhost:3000, breaking sign-in
# through any proxy). Keep it set whenever the app is reached via a proxy;
# it is equally correct for direct localhost dev.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=1

# ── Feature-flag override (optional — see DEPLOYMENT.md §3) ──────────────
#
# NEXT_FLAGS_OFF: comma-separated feature-flag keys forced OFF regardless of
# the FeatureFlag table (the admin flags popover in the header toggles the
# table). It can ONLY turn flags OFF — never on: a default-off flag like `ai`
# is enabled by an admin through the popover or the DB row, never by env.
# Valid keys (src/backend/modules/intel/flags.ts FLAG_KEYS):
#   ai_progress, ai_voice, wallet, marketplace, land_verification, ai
# Intended for local/CI runs that need a deterministic dark surface, e.g.
#   NEXT_FLAGS_OFF=ai_progress,ai
# Unknown keys are ignored, values are case-insensitive, and the flag map is
# cached server-side for 30s — an env change lands on the next read after the
# cache expires (a restart makes it immediate).
# NEXT_FLAGS_OFF=

# ── Optional hardening (all default OFF — see DEPLOYMENT.md §3) ──────────

# SHARE_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS: lifetime in days for client share links
# (/?share=<token> — the sessionless "Virtual Site Visit" bearer capability).
# Default 90 when unset/invalid. Every MINT stamps an expiry: project creation
# and share.regenerate both set now + TTL (rotating also invalidates the old
# token immediately, as before). An expired link is refused exactly like an
# unknown one (same 404/401 copy — no oracle). Tokens minted BEFORE this
# feature (migration 11) have no expiry stamped and keep the old
# never-expires contract until an owner rotates the link; seeds included.
# Valid values: integers >= 1 (anything else falls back to 90).
# SHARE_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=90

# TRUST_PROXY: when set (1/true), the client IP for rate limiting, the
# login lockout and the Daraja IP allowlist is read from the RIGHTMOST
# x-forwarded-for value (the view of a trusted appending reverse proxy),
# defeating XFF spoofing that mints fresh rate-limit buckets. Set only when
# every hop in front of the app is a proxy you control. UNSET (local dev /
# direct exposure — the default) = the x-forwarded-for header is IGNORED
# entirely: every unauthenticated caller shares the ONE 'anon' rate-limit
# bucket, and DARAJA_ALLOWED_IPS (if set) denies everything because the
# source IP is unresolvable (issue #156 — a forgeable header must never
# key a throttle or an allowlist).
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# WEBHOOK_OPEN_POSTURE: explicit opt-in for the OPEN demo/gateway-trust
# posture on the UNAUTHENTICATED field-line webhooks (POST /api/ussd and
# POST /api/whatsapp) when their HMAC secret is unset — i.e. outside
# production only. Posture matrix (secret × runtime × opt-in):
#   secret set (any runtime)        → HMAC gate live (401 without a valid
#                                     X-Signature); this env is irrelevant.
#   secret unset + NODE_ENV=prod    → FAIL CLOSED: POST → 503 before any
#                                     body read. The opt-in is IGNORED in
#                                     production (SEC-4).
#   secret unset + non-prod + this
#     env set to 1/true             → OPEN posture (warn-and-accept): the
#                                     documented demo behavior — real
#                                     attendance rows via applyAction, the
#                                     USSD phone-tail PIN fallback active,
#                                     ONE loud startup warning per route.
#   secret unset + non-prod + unset → FAIL CLOSED: POST → 503 (the DEFAULT
#                                     since issue #156 — staging/preview
#                                     builds and containers started without
#                                     NODE_ENV no longer silently accept
#                                     unauthenticated writes).
# Set it ONLY for local dev/demo runs that exercise the field lines; never
# in production (where it does nothing) and never on a shared host.
# WEBHOOK_OPEN_POSTURE=1

# ── Rate-limit / login-lockout store (optional — DEPLOYMENT.md §9.4) ──────
#
# RATE_LIMIT_STORE: sqlite (default since issue #158) | memory.
#   sqlite = ONE shared SQLite file for every process on ONE host (compose
#            `scale`, PM2, one port per worker): a bucket exhausted on any
#            process is exhausted on all of them, and the login lockout is
#            shared too (issue #33; the DEFAULT). Backed by better-sqlite3 —
#            run the standalone server with node (the Docker CMD does); the
#            Bun runtime cannot load that native addon (it crashes), so the
#            loader detects Bun and falls back to memory with a warning.
#            ANY init failure (unwritable path — e.g. a read-only filesystem
#            or CI container — or a missing module) also logs ONE warning
#            and falls back to memory — the app never fails to boot because
#            of rate limiting. Single HOST only: multi-host needs the Redis
#            implementation of the same seam (not built).
#   memory = the historical in-process counters — exact for ONE app process;
#            each process (or instance) keeps its OWN buckets and lockout
#            state. Opt back in ONLY for single-process dev where the shared
#            db/ratelimit.db sidecar file is unwanted.
# RATE_LIMIT_STORE=sqlite
#
# RATE_LIMIT_SQLITE_PATH: filesystem path of the shared store file (a
# `file:` prefix is tolerated). Default db/ratelimit.db, resolved against
# the process working directory — the same place custom.db lives (db/ in
# dev, /app/db on the Docker volume). NEVER the main Prisma database: it is
# a separate, disposable file (rate-limit state is cache-like; deleting it
# while stopped just resets everyone's limits and lockouts).
# RATE_LIMIT_SQLITE_PATH=db/ratelimit.db

# MUTATION_ORIGIN_ALLOWLIST: optional comma-separated list of EXTRA browser
# Origins (e.g. https://iframe-host.example.com,https://other.example.com)
# allowed to call the mutating JSON routes (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) IN
# ADDITION to the app's own origin. The mutation safety gate is ON by
# default (SEC-1): same-origin browser requests pass, cross-site browser
# requests get 403, and non-browser callers carrying a body must declare
# Content-Type: application/json (415 otherwise — HTML forms and text/plain
# cross-site posts can no longer carry a parseable JSON body). Entries are
# case-insensitive and trimmed; read per request. Leave unset for normal
# same-origin use — set it only when a legitimate page on ANOTHER origin
# must embed/script this app's API.
# MUTATION_ORIGIN_ALLOWLIST=

# USSD_WEBHOOK_SECRET: when set, POST /api/ussd must carry
# X-Signature: <hex HMAC-SHA256 of the raw request body> — the aggregator
# trust model for a real telco gateway. Unset + WEBHOOK_OPEN_POSTURE=1
# (non-production) = open demo posture (PIN attempts are still
# IP-rate-limited; the phone-tail PIN fallback resolves workers). Unset
# otherwise = fail-closed 503 — see the WEBHOOK_OPEN_POSTURE matrix above
# (issue #156).
# USSD_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_SECRET: when set, POST /api/whatsapp (the WhatsApp field
# line seam — same honest-simulation posture as the USSD route) must carry
# X-Signature: <hex HMAC-SHA256 of the raw request body> — shared-secret
# authentication for the relay (Meta Cloud API bridge or aggregator) that
# would POST { from, text, timestamp }. Unset + WEBHOOK_OPEN_POSTURE=1
# (non-production) = open demo posture (requests are still rate-limited
# 20/min/phone + 40/min/IP; the sim footer stays on every reply); unset
# otherwise = fail-closed 503 (issue #156). No WhatsApp provider is wired
# today — GET /api/whatsapp documents the contract.
# WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# WEBSITE_ORIGIN: where the web app proxies /website/* to (the marketing
# site). Default http://127.0.0.1:3001 (same-host). Under docker-compose
# set http://website:3001 (service name).
# WEBSITE_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:3001

# CONTACT_PII_KEY (MD-3, issue #362) — the website's contact-PII encryption
# key. docker-compose.yml interpolates this ONE variable from here into the
# `website` container (the jobs-tick sidecar's pattern — the website never
# sees the app's other secrets); for bare-metal/dev the site reads it from
# mjengoos-website/.env. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32. The site seals
# every submission's PII fields (AES-256-GCM) with it before they touch the
# website-data volume; unset + NODE_ENV=production → the contact form FAILS
# CLOSED (503, nothing written). Full posture + the lead-retrieval /
# erasure write-back commands (scripts/decrypt-leads.mjs):
# mjengoos-website/.env.example + DEPLOYMENT.md §6.3.
# CONTACT_PII_KEY=

# ── Background-job scheduler (optional — DEPLOYMENT.md §7.3) ───────────
#
# JOBS_RUN_TOKEN: shared secret that lets an external scheduler (the
# docker-compose `jobs-tick` sidecar, deploy/systemd/mjengo-jobs.timer,
# any cron) call POST /api/jobs/run without a browser session, via
#   Authorization: Bearer <token>
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# The SAME value must be set on the app (here) and the scheduler.
# UNSET (default) = the bearer path is fully disabled — POST /api/jobs/run
# answers only to contractor/admin sessions, exactly as before. There is
# no default token: fail closed.
# JOBS_RUN_TOKEN=
#
# JOBS_HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS: per-handler timeout for ONE background-job
# invocation during a drain (POST /api/jobs/run) — default 30000 (30 s).
# Generous for the TTS/AI handlers yet far below the route's own duration
# budget, so one hung handler fails its own JobRecord row instead of
# stalling the whole drain. Read at DRAIN time, not import time — change
# it and the next drain picks it up without a restart. A handler that
# exceeds the cap is marked failed TERMINALLY (no retry — it already hung
# a full window and would re-hang; re-enqueue after investigating).
# Invalid, zero or unset values fall back to the default (never 0/NaN — a
# zero cap would fail every handler instantly).
# JOBS_HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#
# ── Health endpoint detail gating (optional — issue #164 / audit API-13) ──
#
# GET /api/health is SPLIT: the public body is the probe minimum
# {"ok":true,"db":"up","timestamp":…} (503 {"ok":false,"db":"down",…} when
# the DB is down) — exactly what the compose healthcheck and the CI smoke
# test assert on. The diagnostics (job-queue counts, entity counts, package
# version, uptime, db latency) are returned only to a caller that passes
# ONE of three gates:
#   1. an ADMIN session (the in-app Overview SystemHealthCard — automatic,
#      zero config);
#   2. the machine header below;
#   3. the HEALTH_PUBLIC_DETAIL demo opt-in below.
#
# HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN: shared secret for ops dashboards / curl that want
# the full diagnostics without a browser session:
#   curl -H "X-Health-Detail: $HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN" http://…/api/health
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32   (same discipline as JOBS_RUN_TOKEN —
# constant-time compare, never commit a real one). UNSET (default) = the
# header path is fully disabled — fail closed, no default token.
# HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN=
#
# HEALTH_PUBLIC_DETAIL: set to 1 (or true) to serve the FULL detail body
# to every unauthenticated caller — the explicit demo posture (a sandbox
# preview or a trusted intranet where the coarse counts were never a
# secret). Deliberate opt-in only: anything else (unset, 0, yes, on…)
# keeps the minimal public shape.
# HEALTH_PUBLIC_DETAIL=
#
# ── Prometheus metrics endpoint (optional — issue #205 / audit OBS-3) ───────
#
# GET /api/metrics serves the scrape signals in Prometheus text format
# (content-type text/plain; version=0.0.4) — the SAME queries /api/health
# runs (db probe latency, job rows by status, uptime, build info) via the
# shared src/backend/lib/health-queries.ts. Point a scraper at it with:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $METRICS_TOKEN" http://…/api/metrics
#
# METRICS_TOKEN: the DEDICATED bearer secret for /api/metrics —
# deliberately NOT JOBS_RUN_TOKEN (that grants background-job DRAIN powers)
# and not HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN (that unlocks health detail incl. DB error
# text): metrics is read-only telemetry with a smaller blast radius, and
# the scraper credential rotates independently of the scheduler's.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32   (constant-time compare, same discipline
# as JOBS_RUN_TOKEN — never commit a real one). UNSET (default) = every
# request 401s, fail closed, no default token. Want one machine secret for
# everything? Set METRICS_TOKEN to the same VALUE deliberately — the
# explicit assignment IS the decision; there is no silent fallback.
# METRICS_TOKEN=
#
# ── Scheduled reconciliation (issue #212 — escrow drift alarm) ─────────────
#
# The POST /api/jobs/run callee (whatever drains it: compose jobs-tick,
# systemd timer, cron) keeps ONE 'reconciliation' job row on the books —
# seeded at most once per interval, never stacked (a queued/retrying row
# blocks the seed; a manual {type:"reconciliation"} POST cannot double-book).
# The job runs the A-1-lite debit-backing check AND, for EVERY escrow wallet,
# the derived-ledger-sum vs EscrowWallet.balance comparison — READ-ONLY, with
# the findings recorded on the JobRecord result JSON (per-project cents).
#
# RECONCILIATION_CHECK_INTERVAL_MIN: minutes between scheduled checks.
# Default 1440 = daily. Invalid values warn once and use the default.
# RECONCILIATION_CHECK_INTERVAL_MIN=1440
#
# ESCROW_DRIFT_ALERT_CENTS: |derived − projected| ≥ this (integer CENTS)
# → one escrow.drift DomainEvent + in-app notifications to BOTH the finance
# and contractor audiences on the drifted project (re-alerts each check
# while drift persists). Default 1 = the Money-tab chip's exact-equality
# convention (issue #122 — a one-cent drift is a drift); raise it ONLY to
# tolerate a known projection quirk while it is being fixed. Sub-threshold
# drift is still recorded in the job result, just not alerted.
# ESCROW_DRIFT_ALERT_CENTS=1

# ── Error sink (observability — opt-in webhook, issue #202 / audit OBS-1) ───
#
# HONEST DEFAULT: UNSET = journal-only, exactly the behavior of every prior
# release. When a route handler, a background-job handler or a webhook
# processor fails, the structured log line (lib/log.ts, issue #204) IS the
# record — nothing external is contacted, and captureError() is a no-op that
# warns ONCE per process that the sink is inactive (never per-call spam).
#
# ERROR_SINK_URL: the gate. When SET, every captured error additionally
# makes ONE fire-and-forget JSON POST to this endpoint (node fetch, zero new
# dependencies — the NOTIFY_SMS_WEBHOOK_URL pattern; a Sentry-class adapter
# can sit behind the same seam later):
#   POST ${ERROR_SINK_URL}
#   Content-Type: application/json
#   Authorization: Bearer ${ERROR_SINK_TOKEN}          // header only if set
#   { "ts": ISO, "service": "mjengo-os", "environment"?: tag,
#     "scope": "api/actions POST" | "jobs" | …,
#     "requestId"?: rid, "route"?: …, "method"?: …,   // the #204 log context
#     "error": { "class", "message", "stack"?, "internal" },
#     "context"?: { "jobType", "jobId", … } }         // capture-site fields
# Contracts: captureError NEVER throws and NEVER blocks the response (the
# POST is detached with a 5s abort bound); Prisma/framework internals are
# redacted before the wire (message replaced, stack omitted — the
# safeErrorMessage discipline); failed POSTs are warned once in the journal
# and dropped — NO retries, no retry storm (the journal line stays the
# durable record). The client IP is deliberately never sent.
# SECRET-CLASS: anyone holding this URL can POST events into your collector
# (Slack-style hook URLs even embed a token in the path) — treat like a
# bearer capability, never commit a real one.
# ERROR_SINK_URL=
#
# ERROR_SINK_TOKEN: optional bearer token sent as the Authorization header
# (when your collector authenticates callers). Secret — same discipline as
# JOBS_RUN_TOKEN. Unset = no auth header (e.g. an internal relay).
# ERROR_SINK_TOKEN=
#
# ERROR_SINK_ENV: optional NON-secret deployment tag ('prod-1', 'staging',
# …) labeling every payload so one collector can serve several deployments.
# Unset → falls back to NODE_ENV; absent from the payload when neither is set.
# ERROR_SINK_ENV=

# ── Notification channels (notify module — SMS providers, both optional) ───
#
# TWO SMS providers, one honest seam — pick ONE (webhook wins if both are
# set; a partial AT pair is ignored, fail-closed). With NEITHER configured no
# external send is ever attempted; every notification row stays
# deliveryStatus 'logged' (in-app only — honest).
#
# CREDENTIAL TRADEOFF (the operator's choice):
#   · Webhook (below): the gateway YOU run holds the provider credentials
#     (Twilio/AT keys, …) — this app only POSTs JSON to it, so no SMS
#     secret ever lives here. Cost: one more moving part to operate.
#   · Africa's Talking (AT_* vars): no relay to run — the app calls AT's
#     REST API directly, which means the AT API KEY lives in this env file.
#     That key can send (and bill) SMS on your AT account: keep the file
#     uncommitted (it is gitignored) and restrict who/what can read it.

# NOTIFY_SMS_WEBHOOK_URL: POST endpoint of your SMS gateway webhook (a relay
# you own, a Twilio-proxy, or an Africa's Talking-style callback). When SET,
# notify() calls that pass opts.sms additionally attempt one real send — the
# notification row honestly records 'sent'/'failed' + deliveryDetail.
# Request contract: JSON { to, text, metadata: { projectId, kind } }; any 2xx
# counts as accepted (body MAY be JSON { "id": "…" } → recorded as ref).
# See src/backend/modules/notify/channels.ts for the full contract.
# NOTIFY_SMS_WEBHOOK_URL=

# NOTIFY_SMS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN: optional bearer token sent to the SMS webhook as
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Unset = no auth header (e.g. when your
# gateway is an internal relay). Never commit real tokens.
# NOTIFY_SMS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# ── Africa's Talking direct provider (alternative to the webhook above) ────
#
# AT_API_KEY + AT_USERNAME (BOTH required — a partial pair is treated as
# unset, fail-closed): when set and no webhook URL is configured, notify()
# calls AT's REST v1 messaging endpoint directly — POST form-urlencoded
# { username, to, message } with the apiKey header; a 2xx response's
# SMSMessageData.Recipients[0].messageId is recorded as providerRef.
# Get the key from your dashboard at account.africaistalking.com. Never
# commit real values.
# AT_API_KEY=
# AT_USERNAME=
#
# AT_SENDER_ID (optional): a registered AT short code / alphanumeric sender
# id sent as the `from` field. Unset → your account's default sender.
# AT_SENDER_ID=
#
# AT_ENV (optional): 'sandbox' points the provider at
# https://api.sandbox.africaistalking.com — AT's sandbox where the username
# is 'sandbox' and no credit is spent — for testing the wiring without
# billing. Any other value / unset = the production endpoint. Same pattern
# as DARAJA_ENV in the wallet module.
# AT_ENV=

# ── Web push (notify module — VAPID pair, optional) ────────────────────────
#
# HONEST SCOPE (W5-1): real browser push behind the same ChannelProvider
# seam as the SMS providers. VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY + VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY are BOTH
# required — a partial pair is treated as unset (fail-closed). With NEITHER
# pair set, browser subscriptions are still STORED (POST /api/push/subscribe
# works, the Settings tab honestly says "not configured"), but every push
# send attempt stays deliveryStatus 'logged' with the honest skip reason in
# the row — nothing is faked, exactly like the SMS channels.
#
# Generate a pair ONCE and keep it for the life of the deployment (rotating
# it invalidates every existing browser subscription):
#   bunx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# (or: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys). The PUBLIC half is served to
# browsers by GET /api/push/subscribe; the PRIVATE half signs every send and
# must stay secret — env-file discipline (never committed, narrow read
# access), same as AT_API_KEY.
#
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
#
# VAPID_SUBJECT (REQUIRED in production): a mailto: or https:// URL the push
# services (Google's FCM, Apple, Mozilla) can contact about your traffic —
# the VAPID spec's abuse-contact. Unset or left at the labeled
# 'mailto:admin@localhost' default, production FAILS CLOSED (issue #354 /
# MD-2): no push is sent (rows stay 'logged' with the refusal reason, the
# Settings tab honestly says "not configured"), one loud error per process.
# Dev/test keeps the labeled fallback with a one-time warning.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ── M-Pesa Daraja (wallet module — PaymentProvider seam) ──────────────────
#
# HONEST SCOPE: a Safaricom Daraja SANDBOX integration behind the existing
# provider seam — real API shapes (STK push / query / reversal), zero live
# credentials, NOT a licensed integration, no real money. When ANY of the
# seven required values below is missing or blank, the M-Pesa rail stays the
# SimulatedProvider (the default — fail closed, nothing changes).
#
# Sandbox credentials: create an app at developer.safaricom.co.ke (consumer
# key/secret), then use the public sandbox shortcode + the passkey shown on
# the "Lipa na M-Pesa Online" API page. ALL of these are secrets — never
# commit real values.
#
# DARAJA_ENV: sandbox (default) | production — selects the API base URL.
# Production is shape-compatible but this integration is uncertified.
# DARAJA_ENV=sandbox
# DARAJA_CONSUMER_KEY=
# DARAJA_CONSUMER_SECRET=
# DARAJA_SHORTCODE=174379
# DARAJA_PASSKEY=
#
# DARAJA_CALLBACK_BASE: the public HTTPS base Safaricom can reach (no
# trailing slash; Daraja rejects http callbacks). The STK CallBackURL
# becomes {base}/api/webhooks/daraja/{secret-segment}. Behind the sandbox
# preview gateway you cannot receive callbacks — leave the whole Daraja
# block unset there.
# DARAJA_CALLBACK_BASE=https://your-public-host.example
#
# DARAJA_WEBHOOK_SECRET: unguessable per-deployment secret (openssl rand
# -hex 32). The webhook path segment is DERIVED from it (sha256, 32 hex
# chars) — the raw secret never appears in any URL. Required: without it
# the callback URL would be guessable, so the provider is not selected.
# DARAJA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# ── Daraja reversal (refund) — SEPARATE credentials, all-or-nothing ───────
#
# The reversal API needs an initiator identity distinct from the STK set:
# DARAJA_INITIATOR_NAME plus DARAJA_SECURITY_CREDENTIAL (the initiator
# password encrypted with Safaricom's org certificate — generate it OFFLINE;
# the raw initiator password never needs to live in this file). When either
# is unset, refund() fails honestly and tells the operator what is missing —
# it never pretends to reverse anything.
# DARAJA_INITIATOR_NAME=
# DARAJA_SECURITY_CREDENTIAL=

# DARAJA_ALLOWED_IPS: optional source-IP allowlist for the callback route
# (POST /api/webhooks/daraja/{secret}) — comma-separated IPv4 CIDRs and/or
# bare IPv4s, e.g. 196.201.214.0/24,196.201.245.7. When SET, a request whose
# resolved client IP (x-forwarded-for, per TRUST_PROXY) matches no entry is
# rejected 403 BEFORE the body is parsed; an unresolvable IP is also
# rejected (fail closed). UNSET (default) = the documented posture:
# unguessable secret path + query-API reconciliation, no IP filtering.
# IPv6 is exact-literal match ONLY (no IPv6 CIDR). Invalid entries are
# logged and ignored — but a set value with ZERO valid entries denies ALL
# traffic (an operator who asked for filtering never silently gets
# allow-all). REQUIRES TRUST_PROXY=1: with TRUST_PROXY unset the
# x-forwarded-for header is ignored wholesale (issue #156), the resolved IP
# is always unresolvable, and a set allowlist denies ALL traffic — sound
# only behind a proxy you control that forwards x-forwarded-for. Safaricom
# publishes no stable IP list — pull the current ranges from their developer
# portal before enabling.
# DARAJA_ALLOWED_IPS=

# ── Daraja pending-intent reconciliation sweep (jobs module) ──────────────
#
# STK initiations that miss Safaricom's callback would stay pending forever;
# the wallet.reconcile job (seeded at initiation, drained by POST
# /api/jobs/run — see DEPLOYMENT.md §7.3) re-drives the SAME callback
# processor (query-API-verified, dedupe-safe — never a second money path).
# All three values are minutes; all are optional and invalid values fall
# back to the default with a logged warning. UNSET = defaults below, and
# with no Daraja env at all the sweep seeds nothing and does nothing.
# DARAJA_RECONCILE_AFTER_MIN=2      # probe intents once they are this old
# DARAJA_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MIN=5   # re-probe spacing (matches the 5-min tick)
# DARAJA_RECONCILE_MAX_AGE_MIN=60   # stop probing; intent stays PENDING (no invented failure/credit)

# ── Object storage (upload module — S3 / Cloudflare R2 / MinIO) ───────────
#
# HONEST DEFAULT: with ALL five required values below unset, photo uploads
# stay on LOCAL DISK (public/photos on the app server, served by the Next
# server) — byte-identical to every prior release. Nothing changes for a
# single-box self-host; leave the whole block unset there.
#
# Set ALL FIVE required values and the upload module switches to the
# S3-compatible driver (path-style URLs, SigV4 presigning implemented with
# node:crypto — no new dependencies). A PARTIAL set is treated as unset
# (fail closed): the app logs one warning naming the missing keys and keeps
# using local disk. R2, AWS S3 and MinIO all work; S3_ENDPOINT examples:
#   AWS S3  https://s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com
#   R2      https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com   (region: auto)
#   MinIO   http://minio.internal:9000
#
# ALL of these are secrets/infra values — never commit real ones.
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_REGION=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#
# S3_PUBLIC_BASE (optional, recommended): the public URL base that fronts
# the bucket (a CDN, or the bucket's own public origin). When set, uploaded
# photo URLs are stable forever. When UNSET and the S3 driver is active,
# recorded URLs are presigned GETs with the SigV4 7-day maximum — they work
# immediately but EXPIRE after a week (the documented tradeoff; see
# DEPLOYMENT.md "Object storage").
# S3_PUBLIC_BASE=
